  "lineage": "Clinical and stage tradition of spoken hypnotic induction descending from Franz Mesmer's eighteenth-century salons through James Braid, the Manchester surgeon who coined 'hypnotism' in the 1840s, into twentieth-century American practice. Milton H. Erickson developed conversational, indirect induction while Dave Elman codified rapid inductions for physicians; both styles rely on monotone, descending-cadence suggestion delivery, progressive-relaxation deepeners, and counted descents. From the 1970s onward the form also became a mass-market audio genre through self-hypnosis cassettes and later digital downloads.",
